1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:07,487
In 2012, a trading firm called Knight Capital lost $440
million in 45 minutes.

2
00:00:07,537 --> 00:00:08,542
The cause?

3
00:00:08,592 --> 00:00:14,376
An old switch in their software got flipped, and it turned on
code nobody had tested in years.

4
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:17,768
We all check how much of our code is tested.

5
00:00:17,818 --> 00:00:20,893
Nobody checks how much of our feature flags are tested.

6
00:00:20,943 --> 00:00:22,859
So I built FlagCov.

7
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:24,841
Quick explainer.

8
00:00:24,891 --> 00:00:27,589
A feature flag is a switch inside your app.

9
00:00:27,639 --> 00:00:31,508
Flip it in Flagsmith, and real users see the change instantly.

10
00:00:31,558 --> 00:00:33,305
No new code shipped.

11
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:36,558
Switches can do more than on and off.

12
00:00:36,608 --> 00:00:38,485
Imagine 100 shoppers.

13
00:00:38,535 --> 00:00:45,243
Flagsmith shows 60 of them the classic checkout, 30 a one-page
checkout, and 10 a brand new express checkout.

14
00:00:45,293 --> 00:00:46,467
Here's the catch.

15
00:00:46,517 --> 00:00:49,409
The tests only tried the versions someone remembered.

16
00:00:49,459 --> 00:00:51,728
Nobody ever checked express.

17
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:53,676
Here's that shop.

18
00:00:53,726 --> 00:00:55,108
The tests are green.

19
00:00:55,158 --> 00:00:56,906
Everything looks safe.

20
00:00:57,667 --> 00:00:59,520
Now let's turn on FlagCov.

21
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:06,447
It watches which versions the tests actually tried, and
compares that with what Flagsmith is really showing customers.

22
00:01:06,497 --> 00:01:07,515
There it is.

23
00:01:07,565 --> 00:01:11,798
10% of real shoppers get express, and no test has ever tried
it.

24
00:01:11,848 --> 00:01:15,002
Big business customers get an untested version too.

25
00:01:15,052 --> 00:01:18,622
And the code even asks for a switch that doesn't exist.

26
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,370
FlagCov doesn't just warn you.

27
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:23,258
It writes the missing test for you.

28
00:01:23,308 --> 00:01:25,381
I add one line, and run it.

29
00:01:25,431 --> 00:01:26,592
It crashes.

30
00:01:26,642 --> 00:01:31,670
1 in 10 shoppers would have hit this bug, and our green tests
never saw it coming.

31
00:01:32,433 --> 00:01:34,130
And this isn't a fake setup.

32
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:43,570
Every time I push code, a real, self-hosted Flagsmith server
starts up, gets filled with these switches, and FlagCov reads
the real settings straight from it.

33
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:46,097
That's the actual run, on GitHub.

34
00:01:46,867 --> 00:01:50,035
Then I tried it on the Flagsmith repository itself.

35
00:01:50,085 --> 00:01:52,288
690 of its tests.

36
00:01:52,338 --> 00:01:55,517
Three switches are only ever tested in the on position.

37
00:01:55,567 --> 00:01:59,059
But in the default settings, those same switches are off.

38
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:02,989
For a team, it shows up right on the pull request.

39
00:02:03,039 --> 00:02:10,437
This is the real comment: which switches the change touches,
how many real users are untested, and the tests to paste in.

40
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,613
Code coverage tells you what your code ran.

41
00:02:13,663 --> 00:02:16,946
Flag coverage tells you what your users will actually see.

42
00:02:16,996 --> 00:02:18,027
FlagCov.

43
00:02:18,077 --> 00:02:21,832
Free, open source, and one pip install away.
